  "question": {
    "text": "The de Broglie wavelength of a molecule in a gas at room temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>300</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. If the temperature of the gas is increased to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>600</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>, then the de Broglie wavelength of the same gas molecule becomes",
